Why is it a graph?
Modeling the problem as a graph problem. The graph is hidden in the problem -> it maybe difficult to detect.

Graph Structure:
A graph consists of nodes and edges.
number of nodes -> n
number of edges -> m

What is a path?
A path leads from a to b through edges of the graph. The length of the path is the # of edges in it.

Cycle characteristics of a path:
A path is cyle if first node is the last node.

Connectivity:
A graph is connected if any two nodes of graph have a path(notice: a path not an edge).

What is component?
Connected path of a graph is called component.

What is a tree?
Tree is a connected graph with n nodes and n-1 edges. There is a unique path between any two nodes of a tree.

Edge Direction
A graph is directed if the edges can be tranversed in one direction only.

Edge weight
In a weighted graph, each edge is assigned a weight. The weights are often interpreted as edge length.

Neighbors and degrees.
Two nodes are neighbors or adjacent if there is an edge between them.

The degree of a node is the number of its neighbors.
+ The sum of degrees in a graph is always 2*m (m is number of edges).


A graph is regular if degree of every node is constant d.
A graph is complete if degree of every node n-1.

In directed graph, the indegree of a node is the number of edges that end at the node (the arrow head is pointed to node)
the outdegree of a node is the number of edges that start at the node. (the shaft of arrow is start from the node)

Colorings:
Each node has the same color so that no adjacent nodes have the same color.

A graph is bipartite if it is possible to color it using two colors.
-> A graph is bipartite when it does not contain a cycle with an odd number of edges.

Simplicity
a graph is simple 
+ if no edges starts and ends at the same nodes
+ no multiple edges between 2 nodes.


Graph Representation:

There are several ways to represent graph. 
The choice of a datastructure depends on size of the graph and the way the algorithm processes it.

Adjacency list representation:
Each node x in the graph is assigned an adjacency list that consists of nodes to which there is an edge from x.

A convenient way to store adjacency lists is to declare an array of vectors as follows:

vector<int> adj[N];
adj[1].push_back(2);
adj[2].push_back(3);
adj[2].push_back(4);
adj[3].push_back(4);
adj[4].push_back(1);

